/-
# Subgroups of p-th powers in finite p-groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 21.137)

Kourovka Notebook, Problem 21.137 (L. Wilson), marked solved in the 21st issue: if the `p`-th powers
in a finite `p`-group form a subgroup, must that subgroup be powerful? That is, for `p ≠ 2`, if the
`p`-th powers in a `p`-group of exponent `p²` form a subgroup, must that subgroup be abelian? For a
`2`-group of exponent `8`, if the squares form a subgroup, must that subgroup be abelian?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.PGroup

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.PthPowersSubgroupPowerfulKourovka21Problem137Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 21.137 (L. Wilson). UnsolvedMath record
UM-00002646 (`KOU-21.137`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

The set of `p`-th powers is the literal set `{x^p}`; a subgroup `P` is powerful when `[P, P] ≤ P^p`
(`p` odd) or `[P, P] ≤ P⁴` (`p = 2`), with `P^m` generated by the `m`-th powers of elements of `P`.
The questions are posed for a prime `p`; both are refuted for every relevant prime, so each target
is stated for some prime and its refutation covers all of them. The exponent-`8` question for `p =
2` is answered by one explicit group of order `128` (the Sylow `2`-subgroup of `S₈`), a finite
check, and is not stated separately.
-/

/-- Kourovka 21.137 (L. Wilson), main question, for some prime `p`: whenever the set of `p`-th
powers of a finite `p`-group `G` is a subgroup `P`, the subgroup `P` is powerful, i.e.
`[P, P] ≤ P^p` for odd `p` and `[P, P] ≤ P⁴` for `p = 2`, where `P^m` is generated by the `m`-th
powers of elements of `P`. -/
def pthPowersSubgroupPowerful : Prop :=
  ∃ p : ℕ, p.Prime ∧ ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G], IsPGroup p G →
    ∀ P : Subgroup G, (P : Set G) = {g | ∃ x : G, x ^ p = g} →
      ⁅P, P⁆ ≤ Subgroup.closure ((fun x : G => x ^ (if p = 2 then 4 else p)) '' (P : Set G))

/-- Kourovka 21.137 (L. Wilson), odd-prime form, for some odd prime `p`: whenever the set of
`p`-th powers of a finite `p`-group of exponent `p²` is a subgroup, that subgroup is abelian. -/
def pthPowersSubgroupAbelianExponentPSq : Prop :=
  ∃ p : ℕ, p.Prime ∧ p ≠ 2 ∧ ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G], IsPGroup p G →
    Monoid.exponent G = p ^ 2 → ∀ P : Subgroup G, (P : Set G) = {g | ∃ x : G, x ^ p = g} →
      ∀ x ∈ P, ∀ y ∈ P, x * y = y * x

/--
Resolved false: for every prime `p` some finite `p`-group has its `p`-th powers forming a subgroup
that is not powerful: for odd `p` Muliarchyk's group of order `p^(3p+1)` and exponent `p²`, whose
`p`-th powers form a non-abelian subgroup of exponent `p`; for `p = 2` the Sylow `2`-subgroup of
`S₈`, whose squares form `C₂ × D₈` (letter of A. Chang). The 21st issue of the Kourovka Notebook
marks 21.137 solved ("No, it need not be"). Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved
Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem
21.137 (L. Wilson); resolution: K. Muliarchyk, A counterexample to Kourovka Problem 21.137 for p =
3, preprint (2026), and A non-powerful subgroup of p-th powers for every odd prime, preprint (2026),
Kourovka Notebook site; letter of A. Chang of 1 August 2026, Muliarchyk (every odd prime), abstract
and Section 1: for each odd prime p an affine group V ⋊ B_p (V = F_p[r]/(r^(2p))) of order p^(3p+1)
and exponent p² whose set of p-th powers is a subgroup isomorphic to UT₃(F_p) × C_p^(p-1),
non-abelian of exponent p, hence not powerful; Kourovka Notebook (21st issue), Problem 21.137: No,
it need not be (p = 3: Muliarchyk, order 3^15; p = 2: the Sylow 2-subgroup of S₈ has exponent 8 and
its squares form the non-abelian, non-powerful subgroup C₂ × D₈, letter of A. Chang),
https://kourovkanotebookorg.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/kourovka_21.137_solution.pdf.
-/
public theorem_wanted pthPowersSubgroupPowerful_refuted : ¬ pthPowersSubgroupPowerful

/--
Resolved false: for every odd prime `p`, Muliarchyk constructs a group of order `p^(3p+1)` and
exponent `p²` whose set of `p`-th powers is a subgroup isomorphic to `UT₃(F_p) × C_p^(p-1)`, which
is non-abelian; the 21st issue of the Kourovka Notebook marks 21.137 solved ("No, it need not be"),
citing this preprint for all odd primes. Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved
Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem
21.137 (L. Wilson); resolution: K. Muliarchyk, A counterexample to Kourovka Problem 21.137 for p =
3, preprint (2026), and A non-powerful subgroup of p-th powers for every odd prime, preprint (2026),
Kourovka Notebook site; letter of A. Chang of 1 August 2026, Muliarchyk (every odd prime), abstract
and Section 1: for each odd prime p an affine group V ⋊ B_p (V = F_p[r]/(r^(2p))) of order p^(3p+1)
and exponent p² whose set of p-th powers is a subgroup isomorphic to UT₃(F_p) × C_p^(p-1),
non-abelian of exponent p, hence not powerful; Kourovka Notebook (21st issue), Problem 21.137: No,
it need not be (p = 3: Muliarchyk, order 3^15; p = 2: the Sylow 2-subgroup of S₈ has exponent 8 and
its squares form the non-abelian, non-powerful subgroup C₂ × D₈, letter of A. Chang),
https://kourovkanotebookorg.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/kourovka_21.137_solution.pdf.
-/
public theorem_wanted pthPowersSubgroupAbelianExponentPSq_refuted :
    ¬ pthPowersSubgroupAbelianExponentPSq

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.PthPowersSubgroupPowerfulKourovka21Problem137Wanted
